[medium] internal/service/llm/anthropic.go:147 (correctness)
Non-HTTP SDK/client failures fall through to ErrInvalidOutput. The design says ErrInvalidOutput is for Anthropic HTTP 400s and malformed/unparseable successful provider responses, while exported sentinels are the retry contract. A DNS failure, connection reset, TLS failure, or other transport error would be reported as a non-retryable/schema-style output problem instead of a transient provider/client failure, so the later River worker can make the wrong retry/discard decision.
Suggested fix: Do not wrap arbitrary non-API errors with ErrInvalidOutput. Add or use a retryable transient/provider-unavailable sentinel for transport/client failures, keep context cancellation/deadline passthrough, and reserve ErrInvalidOutput for HTTP 400s plus successful responses that cannot be parsed as valid JSON. Add a test with an unreachable/closed test server to lock the classification.
Guidance: In internal/service/llm/anthropic.go, fix mapAnthropicError so arbitrary non-Anthropic-API SDK/client errors are not wrapped with ErrInvalidOutput. Keep the existing errors.As(err, *anthropic.Error) handling: 401 -> ErrAuth, 400 -> ErrInvalidOutput, 429 -> ErrRateLimited, 529 -> ErrOverloaded, and leave other HTTP statuses as-is unless there is already a deliberate sentinel for them. Keep context.Canceled and context.DeadlineExceeded passthrough exactly as they are. For the final fallback branch, return a retryable provider/client failure classification instead of ErrInvalidOutput; in this package the closest existing retryable sentinels are ErrRateLimited/ErrOverloaded, so prefer adding a dedicated exported sentinel in internal/service/llm/client.go only if downstream retry classification can be updated at the same time. Do not classify DNS failures, refused connections, TLS failures, EOFs, or SDK transport errors as ErrInvalidOutput. Add/adjust a test in internal/service/llm/anthropic_test.go using a closed or unreachable httptest server/BaseURL to assert the error is not errors.Is(..., ErrInvalidOutput), while still preserving the existing 400 test as ErrInvalidOutput.
